\subsection{Testing a conclusion-only proof interchange}
A survey or interoperability project for educational proof tools must distinguish a proved conclusion from its active assumptions. Consider classical propositional logic and a proposed exchange record that exports only the final formula, discarding the premise context. A source tool has validly derived $P$ from the assumption set $\Gamma=\{P\}$.
\begin{proposition}
Importing every valid source derivation as an unconditional theorem using only its conclusion is unsound. For finite propositional formulas, an explicit context-preserving truth-table check can certify entailment, at the cost of enumerating all valuations.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The derivation $P\vdash P$ is valid because every valuation satisfying $P$ satisfies $P$. The empty-context claim $\vdash P$ fails at the valuation assigning false to $P$. Thus dropping the context changes a valid entailment to an invalid theorem. For the stated checker, list the $n$ variables occurring in the finite premises and conclusion. Evaluate every one of the $2^n$ truth assignments. Accept exactly if, on each assignment where all premises are true, the conclusion is true. Every valuation of all propositional variables restricts to one listed assignment, and formula values depend only on their occurring variables by induction on formula formation. Acceptance therefore implies entailment; conversely a failed assignment is a counterexample to entailment.
\end{proof}
For a worked context-preserving record, premises $P$ and $P\Rightarrow Q$ entail $Q$; the four assignments verify this. This small checker addresses only semantic consequence in finite propositional logic. It does not preserve the order of student actions, hints, proof strategy, feedback, or quantifier binding in richer languages. A final proof also cannot distinguish two histories with the same proof text, one independently produced and one fully supplied by hints; recovering such history requires additional data.

